        {
          "id": "pyq-fam-8-1",
          "number": "Q1",
          "marks": "20 marks · asked 2017 (Q6), 2018 (Q7), 2019 (Q8a), 2020 (Q5), 2021 (Q5), 2022 (Q7) — the paper's favourite",
          "unitNumber": 8,
          "unit": "Unit 8: Dissolution of Muslim Marriage & Triple Talaq (2019 Act)",
          "file": "Family/Divorce_under_Muslim_Law_Unit8_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "ma1",
          "question": "\"Discuss the validity of Talaq under Muslim law with reference to statutory provisions and case law. / Critically examine the law relating to Triple Talaq (Talaq-e-Biddat) in India.\"",
          "modelAnswer": "<div class=\"qhead\"><div class=\"qy\">20 marks · asked 2017 (Q6), 2018 (Q7), 2019 (Q8a), 2020 (Q5), 2021 (Q5), 2022 (Q7) — the paper's favourite</div>\n<div class=\"qt\">\"Discuss the validity of Talaq under Muslim law with reference to statutory provisions and case law. / Critically examine the law relating to Triple Talaq (Talaq-e-Biddat) in India.\"</div></div>\n<div class=\"qbody\"><div class=\"model\"><div class=\"mt\">Model answer — framework: Introduction → Modes &amp; forms → Statutory frame → Judicial evolution (4 cases) → Constitution Bench → 2019 Act → Conclusion</div>\n<h6>I. Introduction</h6>\n<p>Under Muslim law marriage (nikah) is a civil contract; its dissolution is permitted but discouraged — divorce is \"the most disliked of lawful things\" in the Prophet's words. The husband's unilateral power of talaq is \"especially peculiar to Mahomedan law\" (Zohara Khatoon v. Mohd. Ibrahim, (1981) 2 SCC 509). The Indian legal journey has moved from the Privy Council's form-over-substance approach (Rashid Ahmad, 1932) to constitutional invalidation of instant triple talaq (Shayara Bano, 2017) and its criminalisation (Act 18 of 2019).</p>\n<h6>II. Statutory framework</h6>\n<p><b>(i) S. 2, Muslim Personal Law (Shariat) Application Act, 1937:</b> dissolution of marriage \"including talaq, ila, zihar, lian, khula and mubaraat\" is to be decided by the Shariat notwithstanding custom. <b>(ii) S. 2, DMMA 1939</b> gives the wife judicial grounds (see MA-2). <b>(iii)</b> Post-2019: ss. 3–5, Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Marriage) Act, 2019.</p>\n<h6>III. Requisites of a valid talaq (Hanafi law)</h6>\n<ul style=\"margin-left:20px\">\n<li><b>Capacity:</b> Muslim, sound mind, attained puberty (presumed at 15).</li>\n<li><b>Form:</b> oral or written (talaqnama); no particular form of words; express words need no proof of intent; ambiguous words do. Need not be in the wife's presence, but she must be named (Calcutta view).</li>\n<li><b>Contingent talaq</b> is valid (Hanafi; not Shia).</li>\n<li><b>Classical indulgences:</b> talaq valid even under compulsion, in jest or voluntary intoxication (Mulla 315; Rashid Ahmad v. Anisa Khatun, AIR 1932 PC 25 — \"A talak actually pronounced under compulsion or in jest is valid and effective\"). The modern Court has qualified the anger indulgence: talaq in extreme anger with loss of self-control is invalid (Masroor Ahmed).</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>IV. Forms and their validity</h6>\n<p><b>(a) Talaq-us-sunnat — approved:</b> <i>Ahsan</i> (single pronouncement in tuhr + abstinence through iddat; revocable till iddat ends) and <i>Hasan</i> (three pronouncements in three successive tuhrs; first two revocable, third irrevocable) — valid in all schools.</p>\n<p><b>(b) Talaq-ul-bidaat — disapproved:</b> three pronouncements in one sitting/tuhr, or a single irrevocable declaration. Introduced by the Omayyads; \"good in law though bad in theology\" (Mulla 311); not recognised by Shia law; the most prevalent form in India. Classical PC view: immediately effective irrespective of iddat, communication, or the husband's real intention (Rashid Ahmad). <b>Modern transformation:</b> see V below.</p>\n<h6>V. Judicial evolution — the four milestones</h6>\n<ul style=\"margin-left:20px\">\n<li><b>Jiauddin Ahmed v. Anwara Begum (1981) 1 GLR 358 &amp; Rukia Khatun v. Abdul Khalique Laskar (1981) 1 GLR 375</b> (Gauhati HC, Baharul Islam J.): the \"correct law of talaq as ordained by the Holy Quran\" is that (i) talaq must be for a reasonable cause and (ii) preceded by an attempt at reconciliation by two arbiters, one from each family. The \"good in law, bad in theology\" doctrine treats women as chattel, which the Quran \"does not brook\".</li>\n<li><b>Shamim Ara v. State of U.P., (2002) 7 SCC 518:</b> talaq \"has to be pronounced\"; a bald plea of previous talaq in a written statement (or an affidavit in non-inter-partes proceedings) is <b>not</b> proof; the husband must lead evidence of (1) pronouncement, (2) reasonable cause, (3) reconciliation attempt. Adopted the Gauhati formulation and Krishna Iyer J.'s Yousuf Rawther critique of \"unbridled authority to liquidate the marriage\".</li>\n<li><b>Masroor Ahmed v. State (NCT of Delhi), 2008 (103) DRJ 137:</b> triple talaq, even among Sunnis, should be regarded as <b>one revocable talaq</b>; talaq in extreme anger is invalid; and <b>communication is an essential element of pronouncement</b> — an uncommunicated talaq does not take effect.</li>\n<li><b>Shayara Bano v. Union of India, (2017) 9 SCC 1 (22.08.2017, 5 judges, 3:2):</b> talaq-e-biddat is <b>void</b>. Nariman &amp; Lalit JJ.: it is \"law in force\" through s. 2 of the Shariat Act and <b>manifestly arbitrary</b> under Art. 14 (instant, capricious, without attempt at reconciliation). Kurian Joseph J.: it is not an essential religious practice — \"what is bad in theology is bad in law\". Dissent (Khehar CJI &amp; Nazeer J.): uncodified personal law is outside Art. 13; protected under Art. 25; should be met by legislative injunction, not invalidation.</li>\n</ul>\n<h6>VI. The 2019 Act — the present position</h6>\n<p>Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Marriage) Act, 2019: s. 3 — talaq-e-biddat (spoken, written or electronic) is <b>void and illegal</b>; s. 4 — punishable up to 3 years + fine (cognizable on the wife's information, non-bailable); s. 5 — Magistrate-determined allowance and custody of minor children for the wife; s. 7 — compoundable on settlement. Valid divorce now only through ahsan/hasan talaq, khula, mubarat, tafweez or a DMMA decree.</p>\n<h6>VII. Conclusion</h6>\n<p>The law today validates only the Prophet-approved forms of talaq, disciplines every pronouncement by cause, arbitration and communication (Shamim Ara–Masroor line), and abolishes the instant form altogether (Shayara Bano; 2019 Act). The trajectory — from Rashid Ahmad's formalism to constitutional review of personal law — is the classic illustration of personal law being brought into conformity with the Constitution's promise of gender equality (Arts. 14, 15(3), 21, 25 read with 13).</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n<!-- MA 2 -->"
        },
        {
          "id": "pyq-fam-8-2",
          "number": "Q2",
          "marks": "10 marks · asked 2023 (Q8b)",
          "unitNumber": 8,
          "unit": "Unit 8: Dissolution of Muslim Marriage & Triple Talaq (2019 Act)",
          "file": "Family/Divorce_under_Muslim_Law_Unit8_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "ma2",
          "question": "\"Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act, 1939 — state the grounds on which a Muslim wife may obtain a decree for dissolution of her marriage.\"",
          "modelAnswer": "<div class=\"qhead\"><div class=\"qy\">10 marks · asked 2023 (Q8b)</div><div class=\"qt\">\"Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act, 1939 — state the grounds on which a Muslim wife may obtain a decree for dissolution of her marriage.\"</div></div>\n<div class=\"qbody\"><div class=\"model\"><div class=\"mt\">Model answer</div>\n<p><b>Object:</b> Act 8 of 1939 \"consolidates and clarifies\" Muslim law on the wife's suit for dissolution and removes doubts on apostasy. Before it, a Hanafi wife had virtually no judicial remedy; the Statement of Objects invoked the Hanafi jurists' own rule that in hardship the Maliki/Shafii/Hanbali law may be applied — the Act extends such beneficial rules to all Muslim women, of every school.</p>\n<p><b>Section 2 — the nine grounds:</b> a woman married under Muslim law may obtain a decree on any one or more of: <b>(i)</b> husband's whereabouts unknown for <b>4 years</b> (decree suspended 6 months; set aside if he appears and undertakes conjugal duties — proviso (b); s. 3 requires notice to his would-be heirs, incl. paternal uncle/brother); <b>(ii)</b> neglect/failure to provide maintenance for <b>2 years</b> — A. Yousuf Rawther v. Sowramma, AIR 1971 Ker 261: mere fact of non-maintenance suffices; <b>(iii)</b> imprisonment for <b>7 years or upwards</b> (decree only after sentence final); <b>(iv)</b> failure without reasonable cause to perform marital obligations for <b>3 years</b>; <b>(v)</b> impotency at marriage and continuing (1-year cure window); <b>(vi)</b> insanity for <b>2 years</b> or virulent venereal disease (leprosy omitted, 2019); <b>(vii)</b> option of puberty — married by father/guardian before 15, repudiated before 18, marriage not consummated (Mt. Ghulam Sakina v. Falak Sher, AIR 1950 Lah 45 — consummation must be proved as fact; minority cohabitation does not destroy the option); <b>(viii)</b> cruelty in six forms — habitual assault/making life miserable, evil repute, forcing immoral life, disposal of her property, obstruction of religion, and <b>inequitable treatment of multiple wives</b> (cf. Itwari v. Asghari, AIR 1960 All 684); <b>(ix)</b> any other ground valid under Muslim law — covering lian (false charge of adultery), kafat, and even irretrievable breakdown (Amma Khatoon v. Kashim Ansari, AIR 2001 Jhar 28).</p>\n<p><b>Ancillary sections:</b> s. 4 — a married Muslim woman's apostasy/conversion no longer dissolves the marriage by itself; she may still sue under s. 2; proviso — a convert to Islam who re-embraces her former faith stands outside s. 4 (marriage dissolves). s. 5 — dower rights unaffected. <b>Conclusion:</b> the DMMA is the judicial counterpart of talaq: it converted the wife's fragmented rights into a statutory charter, applying \"beneficial provisions of one school to adherents of other schools\" (Masroor Ahmed) — a \"very salutary principle\".</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n<!-- MA 3 -->"
        },
        {
          "id": "pyq-fam-8-3",
          "number": "Q3",
          "marks": "10 marks · asked (Q3a)",
          "unitNumber": 8,
          "unit": "Unit 8: Dissolution of Muslim Marriage & Triple Talaq (2019 Act)",
          "file": "Family/Divorce_under_Muslim_Law_Unit8_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "ma3",
          "question": "\"Explain the Option of Puberty under Hindu and Muslim law.\"",
          "modelAnswer": "<div class=\"qhead\"><div class=\"qy\">10 marks · asked (Q3a)</div><div class=\"qt\">\"Explain the Option of Puberty under Hindu and Muslim law.\"</div></div>\n<div class=\"qbody\"><div class=\"model\"><div class=\"mt\">Model answer</div>\n<p><b>Muslim law:</b> A marriage contracted on behalf of a minor by a guardian is not void; the minor, on attaining <b>puberty</b> (presumed at 15 unless earlier proved), may <b>ratify or repudiate</b> it — the <i>khyar-ul-bulugh</i> or option of puberty. Statutory form: <b>s. 2(vii) DMMA 1939</b> — where she was given in marriage by her father or other guardian <b>before 15</b>, she may <b>repudiate before 18</b>, provided the marriage has not been consummated. Rules from <b>Mt. Ghulam Sakina v. Falak Sher (AIR 1950 Lah 45)</b>: (1) repudiation is by suit; (2) anything done during minority (even cohabitation) does not extinguish the right accruing after puberty; (3) her assent must come after puberty, express or implied (cohabitation after awareness); (4) consummation to bar the option must be with her consent after majority; (5) refusal of medical examination raises no presumption of consummation (following the PC in Atkia Begum); (6) before 1939 no repudiation lay against the father's/father's father's marriage — the Act put all guardians on par. Contrast <b>Rabia Khatoon v. Mohd. Mukhtar Ahmad (AIR 1966 All 548)</b> (restrictive) as noted in Yousuf Rawther.</p>\n<p><b>Hindu law:</b> Under the pre-1956 shastric law an infant marriage was validated on the option doctrine in reverse — refusal to marry on attaining majority annulled it. Under the <b>Hindu Marriage Act, 1955</b>, a child marriage is not void; by the <b>Prohibition of Child Marriage Act, 2006, s. 3</b>: a child marriage is <b>voidable at the option of the contracting child</b> — the party who was a child may petition within <b>2 years of attaining majority</b>; where the <b>male</b> is above 18 and the <b>female</b> a minor, only she gets the option. Decree consequences include maintenance and s. 5 PCMA powers (orders for residence, maintenance until remarriage). Additionally, under <b>s. 13(2)(iv) HMA</b>, a wife married before 15 and who has repudiated the marriage before 18 may seek divorce. <b>Conclusion:</b> both systems protect the minor's free consent — Muslim law through the 15/18 DMMA option, Hindu law through PCMA's voidability with the 18+2 formulation.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n<!-- MA 4 -->"
        },
        {
          "id": "pyq-fam-8-4",
          "number": "Q4",
          "marks": "20 marks · recurring (DMMA/ Divorce theme; pairs with Q8b-2023 &amp; Yousuf Rawther)",
          "unitNumber": 8,
          "unit": "Unit 8: Dissolution of Muslim Marriage & Triple Talaq (2019 Act)",
          "file": "Family/Divorce_under_Muslim_Law_Unit8_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "ma4",
          "question": "\"The words 'has failed to provide' in s. 2(ii) of the Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act, 1939 imply a duty to provide.\" Critically examine this statement with case law.",
          "modelAnswer": "<div class=\"qhead\"><div class=\"qy\">20 marks · recurring (DMMA/ Divorce theme; pairs with Q8b-2023 &amp; Yousuf Rawther)</div>\n<div class=\"qt\">\"The words 'has failed to provide' in s. 2(ii) of the Dissolution of Muslim Marriages Act, 1939 imply a duty to provide.\" Critically examine this statement with case law.</div></div>\n<div class=\"qbody\"><div class=\"model\"><div class=\"mt\">Model answer</div>\n<h6>I. The provision and the problem</h6>\n<p>S. 2(ii) DMMA: the wife may sue where \"the husband has neglected or has failed to provide for her maintenance for a period of two years.\" Clause (iv), dealing with failure of marital obligations, expressly says \"without reasonable cause\"; clause (ii) <b>omits</b> those words. Two questions follow: (1) is failure to maintain actionable regardless of cause (poverty, illness, imprisonment)? (2) is it actionable even where the wife's own conduct — refusal to live with the husband, unchastity — caused the failure?</p>\n<h6>II. The duty view (restrictive)</h6>\n<p>Early courts held the Act did not abrogate the general Muslim law: a husband \"cannot be said to have neglected or failed to provide maintenance for his wife unless under the general Mahomedan Law he was under an obligation to maintain her\" (Chief Court of Sind, 1942). <i>Zafar Hussain v. Akbari Begum</i> (AIR 1944 Lah 336): \"neglect implies wilful failure\"; the words \"has failed to provide\" imply \"an omission of duty\". The Nagpur HC (AIR 1951 Nag 375, Mudholkar J.): the Act \"crystallises a portion of the Muslim law\" and must be read with the whole law — a wife who for no reason stays away from the house where she \"ought properly to be\" cannot claim maintenance there, so no \"failure\". The Rajasthan DB (AIR 1956 Raj 102): failure must be \"without any justification\"; Peshawar and Allahabad (Allsop Ag. C.J., AIR 1947 All 3) similar: the husband \"is not bound to follow his wife wherever she may go and force money or food or clothes upon her\". On this view a disloyal or disobedient wife gets no decree.</p>\n<h6>III. The fact view (beneficent/plain-grammatical)</h6>\n<p><i>Manak Khan v. Mulkhan</i> (AIR 1941 Lah 167, Abdul Rashid J.): since cl. (iv) expressly contains \"without reasonable cause\" and cl. (ii) does not, \"whatever the cause may be the wife is entitled to a decree... even though the wife may have contributed towards the failure.\" <i>Noor Bibi v. Pir Bux</i> (AIR 1950 Sind 8, Tyabji C.J., reaffirming Hajra's case): whether there was failure to maintain is \"a pure question of fact\" not depending on the wife's right to enforce maintenance; a man may \"fail to maintain his wife\" even where she could not in law claim maintenance, just as he may fail to pay \"debts of honour\". The principles of maintenance <i>durante</i> marriage and of dissolution are \"entirely different\": dissolution is granted \"when a cessation of the state of marriage has in reality taken place, or the continuance of the marriage has become injurious to the wife\" — the Quran abhors suspension of marriage and enjoins either kind treatment or release (II:229, 231; IV:129; the Jameela–Thabit ibn Qais khula). Mulla's note: failure need not be wilful — poverty, failing health, loss of work, imprisonment all entitle the wife; even a rich wife needs no independent means.</p>\n<h6>IV. The Kerala resolution — A. Yousuf Rawther v. Sowramma</h6>\n<p>Krishna Iyer J. adopted the Sind view: beneficent legislation for a weaker section must be read with \"social perspective and purpose\"; s. 2(ii) \"does not speak of the wife's right of maintenance but only of the fact of her being provided with maintenance\"; the fear that women may divorce \"by their own delinquency\" is misplaced (Bertrand Russell: \"The easiest divorce laws by no means always produce the greatest number of divorces\"). <b>Holding:</b> \"a Muslim woman, under s. 2(ii) of the Act, can sue for dissolution on the score that she has not as a fact been maintained even if there is good cause for it.\" s. 2(ix) independently preserves prior Islamic rights (khula).</p>\n<h6>V. Assessment</h6>\n<p>The fact view better fits (a) the legislative contrast between cls. (ii) and (iv); (b) the Act's ameliorative object (SOR: \"unspeakable misery to innumerable Muslim women\"); (c) the Islamic conception that dissolution responds to the <i>reality</i> of a dead marriage, not to the husband's fault; and (d) gender parity — the husband needs no cause for talaq, so the wife's 2-year non-maintenance ground needs no proof of his breach. The duty view survives only at the margins (e.g., where her conduct wholly disentitles her to any maintenance, some courts still deny the decree); Mulla's editor too would read in a conduct-limiter. <b>Conclusion:</b> the statement is unsustainable as an absolute proposition; the better view, per Yousuf Rawther and the Sind line, is that s. 2(ii) looks to the fact of non-maintenance — the wife's own conduct is irrelevant to the ground, though it may colour relief and maintenance claims.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n<!-- MA 5 -->"
        },
        {
          "id": "pyq-fam-8-5",
          "number": "Q5",
          "marks": "10 marks · asked (Q1d)",
          "unitNumber": 8,
          "unit": "Unit 8: Dissolution of Muslim Marriage & Triple Talaq (2019 Act)",
          "file": "Family/Divorce_under_Muslim_Law_Unit8_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "ma5",
          "question": "\"What is a fasid (irregular) marriage? State its legal implications.\"",
          "modelAnswer": "<div class=\"qhead\"><div class=\"qy\">10 marks · asked (Q1d)</div><div class=\"qt\">\"What is a fasid (irregular) marriage? State its legal implications.\"</div></div>\n<div class=\"qbody\"><div class=\"model\"><div class=\"mt\">Model answer</div>\n<p><b>Concept (Mulla 264):</b> a marriage which is not valid may be void (<i>batil</i> — unlawful in itself, perpetual and absolute prohibition: consanguinity, affinity, fosterage) or irregular (<i>fasid</i> — unlawful \"for something else\": temporary or relative prohibition, or accidental defect). The five classical <b>irregular</b> marriages: (a) without witnesses; (b) fifth wife; (c) with a woman undergoing iddat; (d) prohibited by difference of religion; (e) <b>unlawful conjunction</b> (two sisters, aunt &amp; niece — <i>jama bain-al-mahramain</i>).</p>\n<p><b>Legal implications (Mulla 267):</b> (1) either party may terminate it, before or after consummation, by words showing intention to separate; (2) it has no legal effect <b>before consummation</b>; (3) if consummated — wife entitled to dower (proper or specified, whichever is less); she must observe iddat (three courses, for divorce or death); <b>issue is legitimate</b>; but no mutual rights of inheritance between spouses. (Shia law treats many Sunni-irregular marriages as void outright.)</p>\n<p><b>Judicial gloss — Chand Patel v. Bismillah Begum, 1 (2008) DMC 588 (SC):</b> whether unlawful conjunction (marriage with the sister of an existing wife) is void or irregular divided the courts — <i>Aizunnissa v. Karimunissa</i> (Cal, 1895: void; children illegitimate) versus <i>Tajbi Abalal Desai v. Mowla Alikhan Desai</i> (Bom, 1917: irregular — the prohibition is temporary, removable by death/divorce of the first wife; supported by Fatawa-i-Alamgiri, Baillie, Ameer Ali, Tyabji, Abdur Rahim), followed by Oudh, Lahore and Madras. The SC adopted the Bombay view: <span class=\"hl\">\"the bar of unlawful conjunction renders a marriage irregular and not void\"</span>; an irregular marriage subsists till terminated in accordance with law; hence <b>both wives are entitled to maintenance under s. 125 CrPC</b>. <b>Conclusion:</b> the fasid category shows Muslim law's middle path — the defect is curable; women and children of such marriages are protected rather than cast out.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n<!-- MA 6 -->"
        },
        {
          "id": "pyq-fam-8-6",
          "number": "Q6",
          "marks": "10 marks · asked (Q1c, Q8a)",
          "unitNumber": 8,
          "unit": "Unit 8: Dissolution of Muslim Marriage & Triple Talaq (2019 Act)",
          "file": "Family/Divorce_under_Muslim_Law_Unit8_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "ma6",
          "question": "\"Write a note on Muta marriage and its legal implications (Nikah vs Muta).\"",
          "modelAnswer": "<div class=\"qhead\"><div class=\"qy\">10 marks · asked (Q1c, Q8a)</div><div class=\"qt\">\"Write a note on Muta marriage and its legal implications (Nikah vs Muta).\"</div></div>\n<div class=\"qbody\"><div class=\"model\"><div class=\"mt\">Model answer</div>\n<p><b>Nature:</b> <i>Muta</i> (literally \"enjoyment\") is a <b>temporary marriage for a fixed period and specified dower</b>, recognised only by the <b>Ithna Ashari (Shia)</b> school; Sunni law does not recognise it. Essentials: fixation of the <b>term</b> (hours, days, years) and of <b>dower</b>; no witnesses required; no divorce needed — it ends by expiry of the term or by <b>hiba-i-muddat</b> (the husband's gift of the unexpired term, which releases the wife for the balance).</p>\n<p><b>Legal implications:</b> (1) children are legitimate and inherit from both parents (mutual inheritance between spouses only if the contract so provides/custom permits); (2) the wife is entitled to the <b>whole specified dower</b> even if the term expires without consummation (contrast nikah: half if unconsummated); (3) <b>no mutual inheritance</b> between the spouses by default; (4) the wife is <b>not entitled to maintenance</b> as of right; (5) she need not observe iddat except in certain cases (and must observe it after expiry if consummated — 2 courses; if the marriage was with a minor/unmarried woman, longer); (6) any number of muta marriages may be contracted (polygamy limits of nikah do not apply); (7) it is a contract of enjoyment, not a continuing status — hence no presumption of conjugal rights or RCR. <b>Position in India:</b> recognised only where the parties follow Ithna Ashari law (Bombay, Madras &amp; Sind choices-of-law historically); <i>Atika Bibi v. Abu Mohammed</i> type disputes turned on proof of the term. <b>Contrast with nikah:</b> permanence vs term; dower default rules; maintenance; inheritance; divorce machinery (talaq/khula) vs expiry/hiba. <b>Conclusion:</b> muta remains a school-specific institution; for Sunnis such a union is either void or treated as nikah if the parties intended permanence.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n<!-- MA 7 -->"
        },
        {
          "id": "pyq-fam-8-7",
          "number": "Q7",
          "marks": "10–20 marks · asked (Q8a, Q6) — Unit VIII/IX bridge",
          "unitNumber": 8,
          "unit": "Unit 8: Dissolution of Muslim Marriage & Triple Talaq (2019 Act)",
          "file": "Family/Divorce_under_Muslim_Law_Unit8_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "ma7",
          "question": "\"Discuss the claim of a Muslim wife to maintenance under s. 125 CrPC and the effect of a plea of talaq on it.\"",
          "modelAnswer": "<div class=\"qhead\"><div class=\"qy\">10–20 marks · asked (Q8a, Q6) — Unit VIII/IX bridge</div><div class=\"qt\">\"Discuss the claim of a Muslim wife to maintenance under s. 125 CrPC and the effect of a plea of talaq on it.\"</div></div>\n<div class=\"qbody\"><div class=\"model\"><div class=\"mt\">Model answer</div>\n<p><b>The claim:</b> s. 125 CrPC gives a divorced wife (not able to maintain herself) a summary right of maintenance against a husband with sufficient means who neglects or refuses to maintain her — designed to prevent vagrancy; Arts. 15(3), 38, 39 inform its beneficent construction (Bai Tahira v. Ali Hussain, AIR 1979 SC 362). After Shah Bano and the 1986 Act, the Muslim divorced woman's post-iddat entitlement runs through the Muslim Women (Protection of Rights on Divorce) Act, 1986, read in <b>Danial Latifi v. UOI (2001) 7 SCC 740</b>: the husband must make a <b>fair and reasonable provision beyond iddat</b> within iddat; the relatives/Wakf Board/state scheme is the fallback — hence the Act survives constitutionally. (Unit IX elaboration.)</p>\n<p><b>The talaq-plea gambit — Shamim Ara v. State of U.P. (2002):</b> husbands routinely plead a past talaq to cut off maintenance. The Supreme Court closed the door: (1) talaq must be <b>pronounced</b> and <b>proved by evidence</b> — \"a mere plea taken in the written statement... cannot by itself be treated as effecting talaq\"; (2) the plea must show <b>reasonable cause</b> and a prior <b>attempt at reconciliation by two arbiters</b>; (3) self-serving affidavits in non-inter-partes proceedings are worthless. Until the husband discharges this burden, the woman remains a \"wife\" for s. 125. <b>Masroor Ahmed</b> adds the communication requirement; <b>Chand Patel</b> shows that even a fasid (irregular) marriage subsists till annulled, entitling its wife to maintenance. <b>Itwari v. Asghari</b> shows the converse weapon: a mala fide RCR suit filed to defeat maintenance will be dismissed as inequitable. <b>Conclusion:</b> proof-crippled pleas of talaq no longer defeat the summary remedy; the burden squarely lies on the husband.</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n<!-- MA 8 -->"
        },
        {
          "id": "pyq-fam-8-8",
          "number": "Q8",
          "marks": "20 marks · asked (Q7a &amp; b; Q1) — Unit VII bridge (needed for divorce answers)",
          "unitNumber": 8,
          "unit": "Unit 8: Dissolution of Muslim Marriage & Triple Talaq (2019 Act)",
          "file": "Family/Divorce_under_Muslim_Law_Unit8_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "ma8",
          "question": "\"State the essentials of a valid nikah under Sunni and Shia law.\"",
          "modelAnswer": "<div class=\"qhead\"><div class=\"qy\">20 marks · asked (Q7a &amp; b; Q1) — Unit VII bridge (needed for divorce answers)</div><div class=\"qt\">\"State the essentials of a valid nikah under Sunni and Shia law.\"</div></div>\n<div class=\"qbody\"><div class=\"model\"><div class=\"mt\">Model answer (condensed)</div>\n<p><b>Common essentials:</b> (1) parties of sound mind and past puberty (15 presumption; minors may be contracted by guardians); (2) free consent — proposal (<i>ijab</i>) and acceptance (<i>qubul</i>) at <b>one meeting</b>, in terms signifying marriage, without conditions defeating it; (3) absence of (i) absolute prohibitions — consanguinity, affinity, fosterage (void), and (ii) relative prohibitions — unlawful conjunction, fifth wife, iddat, difference of religion, absence of witnesses (irregular/fasid); (4) dower (consideration); (5) no polyandry; (6) not in violation of the doctrine of permanent scarcity? — (mainly) the above suffice.</p>\n<p><b>Sunni (Hanafi) specifics:</b> proposal &amp; acceptance in the presence of <b>two adult witnesses</b> (their absence makes it irregular, not void); no wali (guardian) indispensable for an adult; writing unnecessary; no specific formula. <b>Shia specifics:</b> witnesses <b>not required</b> for validity but recitation of the formula is; guardian's (wali's) participation valued; a marriage in defiance of a specific prohibition (e.g., ninth wife in aggregate; marriage during pilgrimage ihram) is void; temporary marriage (muta) recognised. <b>Legal effects of valid nikah:</b> legality of intercourse; children's legitimacy and mutual inheritance; wife's dower and maintenance; wife's observance of iddat; prohibition of a fifth wife simultaneously; lawful consortium — enforced, if need be, by a suit for restitution of conjugal rights, which equity may refuse where the husband's second wife has made cohabitation unjust (Itwari v. Asghari, AIR 1960 All 684).</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n<!-- MA 9 -->"
        },
        {
          "id": "pyq-fam-8-9",
          "number": "Q9",
          "marks": "10 marks · asked (Q1b)",
          "unitNumber": 8,
          "unit": "Unit 8: Dissolution of Muslim Marriage & Triple Talaq (2019 Act)",
          "file": "Family/Divorce_under_Muslim_Law_Unit8_DU_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "ma9",
          "question": "\"Compare prohibited relationships under Hindu and Muslim law.\"",
          "modelAnswer": "<div class=\"qhead\"><div class=\"qy\">10 marks · asked (Q1b)</div><div class=\"qt\">\"Compare prohibited relationships under Hindu and Muslim law.\"</div></div>\n<div class=\"qbody\"><div class=\"model\"><div class=\"mt\">Model answer</div>\n<p><b>Hindu law (s. 5(iv)-(v) HMA):</b> marriage within the <b>prohibited degrees</b> (lineal ascent, spouse of lineal ascendant/descendant, sister/aunt etc. as enumerated) or between <b>sapindas</b> (offering pinda — generally 3 generations ascending through the mother and 5 through the father) is <b>void</b>, unless custom permits (s. 5 proviso). Sapinda extends equally to both sides; half/uterine blood rules apply.</p>\n<p><b>Muslim law:</b> prohibitions divide into (1) <b>absolute/perpetual</b> — consanguinity (mother, daughter, sister, aunt, niece...), affinity (mother-in-law, step-daughter raised by her mother, son's wife's mother...), fosterage (wet-nurse within forbidden degrees) — marriage <b>void (batil)</b>, children illegitimate, no dower; and (2) <b>relative/temporary</b> — unlawful conjunction (two wives so related they could not have intermarried, e.g., two sisters; a fifth wife; a woman in iddat; difference of religion) — marriage merely <b>irregular (fasid)</b>, curable (e.g., by divorcing the first sister), with the consequences stated in Chand Patel v. Bismillah Begum (subsistence till annulled; s. 125 maintenance). <b>Contrast:</b> Hindu law knows no \"irregular\" middle category and bars both sides symmetrically; Muslim law grades defects and preserves the status/legitimacy of issue under fasid marriages; both allow custom a role (HMA expressly; Muslim law subject to the Shariat Act's override of contrary custom).</p>\n</div></div></div>\n\n<div class=\"card\">\n<h4>Exam strategy for Unit VIII</h4>\n<ul class=\"tick\">\n<li><b>20-markers:</b> Talaq validity/triple talaq (prepare MA-1 verbatim structure); DMMA essay (MA-2 expanded with s. 2(ii) debate = MA-4). Always quote: Shariat Act s. 2; DMMA s. 2 clauses with periods; at least 4 cases with one-line ratios.</li>\n<li><b>10-markers:</b> option of puberty (MA-3), fasid marriage (MA-5), khula vs mubarat, communication/proof of talaq (Fig. 4), lian, tafweez.</li>\n<li><b>Case-name bank to drop in:</b> Rashid Ahmad (PC, 1932) · Ghulam Sakina (1950) · Itwari (1960) · Yousuf Rawther (1971) · Jiauddin/Rukia Khatun (1981) · Shamim Ara (2002) · Masroor Ahmed (2008) · Chand Patel (2008) · Shayara Bano (2017) · Danial Latifi (2001, Unit IX).</li>\n<li><b>Structure every answer:</b> definition → statutory provision → classification → case law → current law (2017/2019) → conclusion. Draw the small flowcharts (Figs. 2/4/7) in the margin — examiners reward them.</li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n</div>"
        }
