          "unit": "Topic 9: Adjudicatory Bodies (NCLT, NCLAT & Special Courts)",
          "file": "sem 3/company/DU_Topic_9_Adjudicatory_Bodies_Comprehensive_Notes.html",
          "anchorId": "case-u9-2",
          "facts": "This was not a dispute about one company’s debt, share register or winding up. The facts concern the legislative design of institutions : The Companies (Second Amendment) Act, 2002 inserted provisions in Parts IB and IC of the Companies Act, 1956 for NCLT and NCLAT. Madras Bar Association challenged the transfer of jurisdiction and the bodies’ constitution, qualifications and service arrangements before the Madras High Court. On 30 March 2004 , the High Court upheld the creation of the specialist system in principle but identified structural provisions offending separation of powers. Defects had to be removed before the bodies could constitutionally exercise the transferred jurisdiction. Both sides appealed: the association objected to the acceptance of tribunal creation; the Union objected to the adverse findings on structure. The 2010 Constitution Bench upheld the creation of both bodies and agreed that significant corrections were needed. Paragraph 120 tabulated the safeguards. Parliament enacted the Companies Act, 2013 , with Chapter XXVII governing NCLT/NCLAT. The association alleged that important defects condemned in 2010 had reappeared in the new legislation. The present writ petition challenged the new scheme, particularly provisions identified in the prayer, as contrary to Article 14 and constitutional judicial-independence requirements. A related WP 267/2012 was not finally decided with this petition: it required a further Government response. The Court proceeded separately with WP 1072/2013. Procedural posture: constitutional adjudication by the Supreme Court, not an appeal under section 421 or 423 against an NCLT/NCLAT merits order. At the close of the judgment, the Court expressly noted that the tribunals’ functioning had not yet started .",
          "issues": "Petitioner’s argument, as recorded in DU Senior counsel Arvind P. Datar made no serious renewed attempt to overturn NCLT’s validity, acknowledging the 2010 decision. The principal institutional attack was on NCLAT . Although its validity had also been upheld in 2010, counsel argued that there had been no adequate independent discussion of it; the conclusion should therefore not foreclose a fresh challenge. The petitioner relied on the 2014 Madras Bar Association decision striking down the National Tax Tribunal (NTT) scheme , arguing that similar treatment should follow for NCLAT. The larger objection to tribunalisation invoked impartiality, fairness, rule of law, separation of powers and the judicial nature of the work being transferred. Pleading nuance: The Court noted that the prayer expressly attacked section 408 but conspicuously omitted section 410, despite the oral emphasis on NCLAT. This was not the sole ground of rejection; the Court examined the earlier decision and the proposed NTT analogy. Respondent’s position and the Court’s response The Union defended the statutory institutions. The DU extract’s detailed treatment centres on the binding force of the 2010 judgment , rather than setting out a separate lengthy Union speech for every sub-issue. It would be inaccurate to manufacture one. The Court held that the 2010 litigation had expressly included and decided NCLAT’s validity . The petitioner had challenged both institutions on common grounds, the earlier Court had examined those arguments, and the operative conclusion specifically upheld both. The association could not strip that conclusion of binding force by saying one institution had received insufficient separate discussion. Two related legal reasons mattered: Res judicata/finality between the parties: the earlier institutional challenge had been decided. Binding precedent and coordinate-bench discipline: the later Bench could not treat the earlier Constitution Bench’s explicit ruling as if it did not exist. These principles do not make every later, altered statutory provision immune from scrutiny. Indeed, the Court went on to test the 2013 provisions against the standards already laid down. They prevent the same resolved institutional issue from being relitigated as though no binding decision existed. Why the National Tax Tribunal analogy failed The Court distinguished the nature of jurisdiction : National Tax Tribunal comparison NCLT/NCLAT system NTT would replace High Court adjudication of substantial questions of law in the tax appellate setting NCLT deals with fact and law at first instance in assigned company matters A constitutional assessment depends on the precise judicial work transferred NCLAT supplies the first appellate layer on fact/law in that specialist framework The 2014 ruling concerned that different institutional design Companies Act 423 preserves an SC appeal on a question of law from NCLAT The lesson is not that technical adjudicators can decide any appeal. It is that one must compare functions, hierarchy and safeguards , rather than reason that invalidation of one tribunal automatically invalidates all tribunals. Holding on Issue 1 The renewed challenge to the constitutional creation of NCLT/NCLAT failed. The 2010 validation stood. The Court did not abolish NCLAT or transfer all of its future appeals back to High Courts.",
          "arguments": "The association argued that Parliament had effectively repeated qualifications disapproved in 2010. The earlier directions required standards appropriate to the judicial functions transferred, including seniority for the civil-service technical route and genuinely relevant specialised experience. Appointment criteria could not be diluted simply because a new Act replaced the old one.",
