          "anchorId": "pyq-u6-9",
          "question": "A Ltd. posted AGM notices 28 days before, but two members never received theirs. At the meeting: quorum fell below five mid-way; the chairman, losing a removal poll, declared the meeting dissolved and left; the remaining four elected a chairman, continued, and passed the removal — afterwards a lone member seeks to undo everything. Advise.” DU-pattern composite — problem format",
          "modelAnswer": "\n\n> 🎯 **Marking Scheme & Issues:** Marks split: № 2 notice limb · № 2 quorum limb · № 2 chairman-dissolution limb · № 2 removal-resolution validity + conclusion.\n\n**Issue 1 — notice:** 28 days from posting easily satisfies the 21-*clear*-day rule even with the 48-hour deemed-service addition (*Balwant Sethi* arithmetic). Non-receipt by two members: the Act operates on *proper despatch* — but proof of posting is not conclusive (*Hardeep Kaur*); if the company proves correct despatch, isolated non-receipt is generally a directory lapse not vitiating the meeting (*Shailesh Harilal Shah*), absent prejudice-cum-design (*Harben v. Phillips* applies to wholesale omission). **Issue 2 — quorum:** S.103 requires 5 (listed-scale aside); *Hartley Baird* validates what passed while quorum existed, but *London Flats* invalidates post-collapse business; on the stricter (and safer) view, what the four passed after the fall is vitiated — UNLESS the meeting was “adjourned” by the articles/continuance doctrine, which cannot rescue *that same session*. **Issue 3 — chairman's dissolution:** he cannot dissolve at will to dodge defeat (*Narayana Chettiar v. Kaleeswara Mills*); the members' election of a new chairman was valid — *provided quorum existed*. **Issue 4 — removal resolution:** director-removal needs S.115 **special notice** + natural-justice hearing (S.169); if the special-notice cycle wasn't run, the resolution fails on that ground alone (*Queens Kuries*) regardless of the meeting's validity; requisitionists need no reasons (*LIC v. Escorts*). **Conclusion:** the meeting itself likely stands (notice sound), the chairmanship succession is valid, but the four-person continuation sits on the London Flats fault-line and the removal resolution collapses for want of special notice; advise the member that his strongest points are the quorum-collapse frame and the S.115 defect — while the company's best answer is Hartley Baird + proof of proper despatch."
