          "question": "Discuss the law relating to prevention of oppression and mismanagement, as a protection of minority shareholders under the Companies Act, 2013. When can the Tribunal grant relief and what orders may it pass?” mapped from Dec-2024 Q5 checklist",
          "modelAnswer": "\n\n> 🎯 **Marking Scheme & Issues:** Marks split: № 2 concept framing (majority rule → qualified minority rights) · № 2 S.241 grounds + mismanagement limb · № 2 dual test + threshold S.244/waiver · № 2 S.242 order-menu + Ss. 243–246 + cases (Shanti Prasad → Radharamanan → Cyrus).\n\n**Framing.** Corporate democracy vests control in the majority forum, but Company Law corrects its excesses at two levels — judge-made exceptions to Foss v. Harbottle, and the statutory chapter XVI (Ss. 241–246), an “ingenious remedy” whose mere availability deters management. These are **qualified minority rights** — open to “any member” (even a majority group, where control lies elsewhere), screened by threshold numbers. **Grounds (S.241):** (a) affairs conducted — or having been conducted — prejudicially to public interest, oppressively to any member(s), or prejudicially to the company (the **oppression/prejudice track**, requiring the Shanti Prasad course: continuous, burdensome-harsh-wrongful conduct with a lack-of-probity/fair-dealing element, suffered qua member); (b) a **material change** in management or control (excluding creditor/shareholder-class–beneficial changes) making future prejudice **likely** (the **mismanagement track**, relief running to the company). **The dual test (S.242(1)):** the Tribunal must opine BOTH that limb-1 conduct exists *and* that facts justify a just-and-equitable winding up while winding up would unfairly prejudice the members — Indian law's deliberate extra stringency over UK S.994 (*TCS v. Cyrus*, ¶16.2). **Standing (S.244):** 100 members / 1/10 of members (whichever less) / 1/10 issued capital; 1/5 for no-capital companies; consent in writing; joint holders one; **Tribunal may waive** if just and equitable (granted in *Cyrus*; standing frozen at filing — *Rajahmundry*). The Central Government may itself apply under S.241(2). **Powers (S.242):** any order to **end the matters complained of** — future-conduct regulation, **buy-outs** by members/company (with capital reduction), transfer restrictions, agreement terminations (with notices; S.243 consequences), setting aside fraudulent transfers, recovery of undue gains — plus interim orders, board supersession/administrators (*Rajahmundry*), and order-driven article alterations that bind as if the company's own (no inconsistent reversal without leave). **Calibration by the Supreme Court:** relief may issue even without a formal oppression-finding where the company's interest demands and deadlock strangles it (*Radharamanan*, echoing *Needle Industries*' “not powerless”), but removal of a director per se is never oppression and S.242 is forward-looking — no implied reinstatement (*Cyrus*). **Conclusion:** Ch. XVI is a salvage jurisdiction: its remedial generosity serves one end only — **“bringing to an end the matters complained of”**, protecting the minority without burying the company in which they invested."
